# Obsidian CS Knowledge Base — Agent Procedure

Vault owner: Akash Singh. **Read `CONVENTIONS.md` in the vault root before writing
anything.** It is the authoritative spec; this is the operating summary.

## Your Role

The user will say only *"convert module 12"* or *"add this to my notes"*. **You** find
every piece of extractable knowledge and route it. **Never ask the user where something
belongs** — the destinations are fully specified below.

## Two Layers

- **Record** — `LLD/Course/NN - <Original OneNote Page Title>/`. Written once.
  One OneNote page = one module = one **folder**. The folder holds numbered MD files:
  `01 - Problem Statement.md`, `02 - <Topic>.md`, etc. Split on natural topic boundaries —
  no fixed file count. Holds narrative, instructor's framing, full problem walkthrough,
  the user's questions.
- **Accumulators** — everything else. Grown across the entire course.

## Extraction Checklist — run on every module

| Look for | Destination |
|---|---|
| Design pattern taught or applied | `LLD/Patterns/{Creational,Structural,Behavioural}/<Name>.md` |
| Principle invoked or violated | `LLD/Principles/<Name>.md` (one note per principle) |
| OOP mechanism, relationship type, UML notation | `LLD/Foundations/<Name>.md` |
| Naive solution and why it failed | *Motivation* section of the pattern note |
| Concurrency concern | `LLD/Concurrency/<Name>.md` |
| Java or Spring Boot idiom | `LLD/Tech/Java/` or `LLD/Tech/Spring Boot/` |
| Interview-relevant remark | `LLD/Interview Approach.md` |

Unmatched content stays in the relevant module file. That residue is correct.

## Merge Rules — critical

Accumulators already hold knowledge from earlier modules. **Search the vault for an
existing note before creating one.** Merge into it; never overwrite. Keep contradictions
side by side under a `[!question]` callout. Append to `## Sources`, never replace.

## Always

- Cross-link both directions: module file → accumulator, and accumulator `## Sources` → specific module file (e.g. `[[03 - Simple Rule Engine/02 - Naive Solution]]`)
- Update the `README.md` index of every folder touched
- Report what was created, updated, and skipped

## Source Material

OneNote PDFs have **no text layer** — ink is rasterised. Render to images and read
visually using `C:\Users\akash\onenote-tools\` (`onenote_to_png.py` at ~35 DPI for an
overview, then `crop_region.py` at ~170 DPI per region; ~16–20 crops per page).
`*.pdf` is gitignored — never commit source exports (~85 MB each).

Code: link the course repo (`github.com/singhsanket143/Design-Patterns`). Inline only
5–20 line teaching excerpts. Never transcribe boilerplate from photographs.

## Voice

Write in Akash's natural language — how he'd explain it to himself, not a textbook.
The test: would the sentence connect in his head immediately?
Bad: "Interchangeable algorithms swapped at runtime"
Good: "You have a finite family of algorithms that all solve the same problem and need
to swap between them at runtime based on conditions — without the caller knowing"
Use analogies where they land better than precision.

## Format

```
---
tags: [lld/patterns, status/draft]
created: YYYY-MM-DD
---
# Title
> [!abstract] One paragraph: what this is and why it matters
```

Callouts: `[!abstract]` open, `[!tip]` insight, `[!warning]` gotcha, `[!danger]` failure,
`[!info]` context, `[!example]` worked example, `[!question]` unresolved.

Diagrams: Mermaid first — `classDiagram` for LLD, `stateDiagram-v2` for state machines,
`flowchart TD` default. Node labels 2–4 words. Excalidraw only when text genuinely fails.

Style: `==highlight==` sparingly, complexity in backticks, link concepts inline,
end with `## Related Notes`. No filler.
